---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = 'Omnisend vs retired Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify continuity (2026)';
const desc = 'A historical Yotpo Email & SMS continuity decision: evaluate Omnisend, preserve permission and order truth, and test campaign ownership before sending.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I choose Yotpo Email & SMS for a new campaign?",
    "answer": "No. Yotpo retired Email and SMS; this is a historical continuity comparison. Its current SMS notice says the dashboard and background services are inactive."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty retired too?",
    "answer": "No. Those are separate current products. Confirm their integration fields and permissions with your chosen sender; loyalty membership is not SMS consent."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Omnisend a useful replacement candidate?",
    "answer": "When one team wants email, SMS, and eligible web push workflows, and the account, plan, countries, sender setup, and event tests meet its needs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I still export the old account?",
    "answer": "Do not assume access. Inventory lawful records already held and ask Yotpo what, if anything, remains recoverable. Missing permission evidence means no promotional import for that channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I keep the same sending number?",
    "answer": "Portability is not promised. Obtain written confirmation from both relevant providers and test registration, replies, and opt-outs before publishing a sender change."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I add a separate email or SMS tool?",
    "answer": "No. Choose ownership after testing the jobs. A single qualified sender may suffice; multiple tools require shared suppression and explicit send ownership."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should recent purchases stop promotions?",
    "answer": "Replay purchase events during delays and immediately before dispatch. Require one customer and order identity, cross-channel exits, and no duplicate send when an event retries."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens if the cutover fails?",
    "answer": "Pause new dispatch, retire queued duplicates, preserve opt-outs, and reconcile event logs. Roll back configuration or hold sends; do not reactivate retired Yotpo services."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use matched hypothetical eligible cohorts and the same time window. Compare incremental net sales less goods, fulfillment, message, platform, support, and migration costs; count discounts and returns once."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Omnisend vs Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Historical comparison · checked November 25, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Omnisend vs retired Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose a current campaign owner and preserve the evidence behind every send. Yotpo Email and SMS are retired products; they are no longer an active alternative for this decision.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>What changed, and what remains current</h2>
      <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">September 2025 migration announcement</a> scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current lifecycle notice</a> confirms that the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Do not plan a new flow, ongoing sending, or an accessible export around that retired service.</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.yotpo.com/platform/">Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty</a> remain separate current products. Keep their useful review and tier data where appropriate, but verify the destination integration and field semantics. A review, points balance, or loyalty tier does not confer channel permission.</p>
      <p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2871974-create-your-omnisend-account">Omnisend currently supports email, SMS, and web push workflows</a>. It is a positive candidate when a team wants coordinated channels and can validate its event and audience needs. Check account approval, plan limits, destination countries, sender registration, and integration behavior. <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2608314-get-started-with-push-notification-channel">Web push eligibility</a> depends on HTTPS, supported browsers/devices, and browser permission; it is not universal reach.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Current Omnisend candidate</th><th>Historical Yotpo continuity</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Campaign ownership</td><td>Consider one owner for tested email/SMS and eligible push jobs.</td><td>Reconstruct required jobs from lawful retained records; no active sender recommendation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Customer data</td><td>Validate identity, permission, suppression, and order events before enabling flows.</td><td>Inventory records already held; ask the supplier about recovery without assuming availability.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Number and replies</td><td>Confirm supported registration and reply handling for each destination.</td><td>No portability promise; obtain written provider confirmation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tool count</td><td>One platform may be sufficient if it passes the jobs.</td><td>Retain Reviews/Loyalty only on their own merits; no mandatory specialist stack.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <h2>Permission, identity, and order truth before activation</h2>
      <p>Keep a permission ledger per channel with source, timestamp, disclosure, scope, and revocations. Preserve unsubscribes and suppression reasons; an import must never turn an unknown or opted-out contact into a subscriber. Validate jurisdiction, recipient timezone, quiet hours, and sender requirements with the responsible reviewer rather than applying one universal legal rule. Resolve ambiguous records before sending.</p>
      <p>Map stable Shopify customer and order IDs, normalized addresses and phone numbers, guest checkouts, profile merges, cancellations, refunds, and subscription status. Keep channel permission separate even when identities merge. Use Shopify order truth for purchase exits and reconcile event timestamps against ingestion times; a segment refresh alone is not evidence that a queued cart promotion will stop.</p>
      <h2>Fourteen proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>Every merchant name and scenario below is fictional. These are proposed acceptance fixtures, not observed merchant results, supplier benchmarks, or proof that any account already supports the configuration. Thresholds are test inputs requiring operator approval. Each job must pass permission, purchase-exit, and retry tests before activation.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: Subscription exclusion</h3>
      <p>Exclude active subscribers from the flash-offer cohort; a delayed subscription update must block dispatch.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio: Launch without a discount</h3>
      <p>Schedule educational email before an engaged-browser launch text; reject unapproved incentives and exit purchasers.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim: One-of-one style targeting</h3>
      <p>Match saved style preference to the available SKU; a sold item must cancel its queued message.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy: Athlete flavor drop</h3>
      <p>Use an approved collaboration audience; keep the email-only shaker offer out of SMS copy and verify both permissions.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame: Collector scarcity</h3>
      <p>Use actual edition stock and verified order count for collector eligibility; stale sell-through data must hold the send.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: Seasonal restock waitlist</h3>
      <p>Separate swell-forecast education from SKU waitlist alerts; alert only a consented matching waitlist while inventory remains.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective: VIP early access and caps</h3>
      <p>Test a Thursday release with a proposed two-hour VIP window; enforce a proposed two-broadcast weekly cap across all send owners.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX: Size-run priority queue</h3>
      <p>Preserve size preference and queue order; stop sold-out sizes and release the general announcement only after inventory reconciliation.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co: Venue timezone routing</h3>
      <p>Match city and recipient timezone to the tour date; enforce a proposed three-text city cap and hold unknown timezone records.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: Value segments and holdout</h3>
      <p>Use verified repeat orders or a proposed $400 lifetime-value threshold, with a randomized eligible holdout; disclose pressing quantity truthfully.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick: Holiday gifting calendar</h3>
      <p>Separate gift-bundle and self-purchase education cohorts; validate shipping deadlines by destination before scheduling.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear: Clearance offer restraint</h3>
      <p>Limit a proposed clearance discount to the bargain-sensitive cohort; compare against a matched holdout without discounting full-price loyalists by default.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab: Batch scarcity and send budget</h3>
      <p>Trigger only from current batch inventory; test a proposed 200-bottle threshold and a one-launch plus one-scarcity message budget.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics: Kiln allocation and maker story</h3>
      <p>Test a hypothetical 4,200-person waitlist for 80 pieces; preserve allocation order, separate maker-story email, and prevent overselling through queued texts.</p>
      <h2>Cutover, approvals, and rollback</h2>
      <p>Name one dispatch owner per job and channel. Inventory retained campaigns, schedules, retries, queued events, links, codes, and integration credentials. Retire obsolete schedules and duplicate queues in systems still controlled; record any retired-service state that cannot be inspected as an unresolved dependency. Never replay an old queue simply because its audience imported successfully.</p>
      <p>In a controlled pilot, test an order arriving during a delay, just before dispatch, and after a retry. Require purchaser exits across promotional email and SMS, opt-outs applied before release, and an idempotency key combining customer, event, job, and channel. Retry the same event and require at most one authorized send. Reconcile inventory and offer expiry at dispatch. Validate STOP/HELP and reply ownership where applicable; keep failed records quarantined with an accountable reviewer.</p>
      <p>Marketing approves copy, incentives, audience, and caps; the data owner approves permission and event mapping; operations approves inventory and fulfillment; the launch owner records acceptance and support handoff. Start with a small eligible cohort and holdout. Handoff includes job owners, timestamps, escalation contacts, suppression ledger, deduplication log, and pause controls.</p>
      <p>If exits, identity, replies, or inventory fail, pause dispatch and cancel remaining queued work before repair. Preserve revocations and deduplication state, reconcile orders, then rerun fixtures. Rollback means restoring a tested current configuration or holding sends, never restarting retired Yotpo services. Do not promise recovered exports or number transfer as a rollback path.</p>
      <h2>Matched hypothetical economics</h2>
      <p>No supplier prices or measured lift are asserted here. For a proposed 30-day experiment, randomly allocate 1,000 equally eligible customers to treatment and 1,000 to holdout using the same eligibility rules, offer policy, and order window. Suppose treatment net sales are $6,000 and holdout net sales are $5,000, already after discounts and returns. Incremental net sales are $1,000.</p>
      <p>With hypothetical incremental goods and fulfillment of $400, messaging and carrier costs of $100, platform allocation of $80, support of $20, and migration allocation of $100, incremental contribution is $1,000 − $400 − $100 − $80 − $20 − $100 = $300, or $0.30 per treatment-eligible customer. Count discounts and returns once. Include all billable attempts and every overlapping tool's allocation; report uncertainty rather than treating attributed revenue as incremental lift.</p>
      <p>Use current written quotes for actual account tiers, destination mix, message segments, credits, overages, carrier charges, onboarding, and data retention. Compare current supplier configurations at the same workload and peak volume; retired Yotpo is not a purchasable price comparator. Keep historical invoices only as historical cost evidence. See <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>.</p>
      <h2>Decision</h2>
      <p>Shortlist Omnisend if coordinated email/SMS and eligible push match the team's jobs and the account passes these tests. Choose a different current configuration if requirements fail; do not force multiple tools or an unsupported ranking. Preserve useful Reviews/Loyalty data with verified integration and independent permissions. Hold campaigns whose evidence is incomplete until the owner resolves the gap.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend and historical Yotpo Email &amp; SMS FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
